    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "tip",
      "title": "Compounds copy the base verb",
      "text": "A verb built on another one copies its participle: <i>deshacer</i> : deshecho, <i>componer</i> : compuesto, <i>suponer</i> : supuesto, <i>prever</i> : previsto, <i>reescribir</i> : reescrito, <i>satisfacer</i> : satisfecho."
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "warn",
      "title": "haber is not tener",
      "text": "<b>Haber</b> is only the helper verb before a participle (<i>he comido</i>). To say you possess something, use <b>tener</b> (<i>tengo un coche</i>). Never write <i>tengo comido</i> for the perfect."
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "dutch",
      "title": "No zijn, no splitting",
      "bullets": [
        "Dutch uses <i>hebben</i> or <i>zijn</i>: <i>ik ben gegaan</i>. Spanish always uses <b>haber</b>: <b>he ido</b>.",
        "Dutch splits the verb: <i>ik heb gisteren gegeten</i>. In Spanish <b>he</b> and the participle stay together: <b>ya he comido</b>, <b>no he comido</b>."
      ]
    },
