    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "dutch",
      "title": "Dutch uses the perfect for both",
      "text": "<i>Ik heb gisteren gegeten</i> and <i>ik heb vandaag gegeten</i> look alike in Dutch. In Spanish, <b>gisteren</b> needs the preterite (<i>Ayer comí</i>) and <b>vandaag</b> needs the perfect (<i>Hoy he comido</i>)."
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "spain",
      "title": "Spain vs Latin America",
      "text": "In Spain the perfect is the normal tense for <b>hoy</b>, <b>esta semana</b>, <b>este año</b> and recent news: <i>¿Has comido?</i> <i>Hoy he trabajado mucho.</i> Many Latin American speakers use the preterite there instead: <i>¿Comiste?</i> <i>Hoy trabajé mucho.</i> Both are understood everywhere, but learn the Spanish pattern above."
    },
